body:
- Motivated by https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12515

We have 5 similar [hashing containers](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/contributing/development/cpp_usage_guidelines.html) in Core. 
This is confusing, because the trade-offs are [not clear](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12515). This PR removes `OAHashMap`, in favour of the nearly identical (but faster) `AHashMap`.

## Comparison

When `OAHashMap` was [first added](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/11405), it was the fastest hashing container, because it used round-robin probing and contiguous data allocations.
Since then, `HashMap` [was reimplemented in 2022](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/60881) with round-robin probing, making it more competitive with `OAHashMap` again.

Additionally, `AHashMap` [was added in 2024](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/92554). It makes [nearly the same decisions](https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12515) as `OAHashMap`. 
It wasn't questioned back then, but because the two types make nearly the same decisions, `AHashMap` should have been a re-implementation of `OAHashMap`. Now, we have two nearly identical data types.

There's 3 notable differences, though:
- `AHashMap` has an interface mostly consistent with `HashMap` (our most common hashing data type). `OAHashMap` has an inconsistent interface.
- `OAHashMap`'s implementation is less well optimized than `AHashMap`'s (see benchmarks).
- `OAHashMap` uses separate value and key arrays (discussed below).

Due to these factors, I think it is appropriate to replace `OAHashMap` entirely, to alleviate our maintenance burden.

## Dense `KeyValue` array vs sparse `Key` array + `Value` array

The only theoretical difference between `OAHashMap` and `AHashMap` is that `AHashMap` stores its values in one dense `KeyValue` array, while `OAHashMap` stores theirs in two separate, sparse arrays.

- `AHashMap`'s `KeyValue` array can be iterated densely. Because the iteration is contiguous, it is fast. `OAHashMap` has to check every next value for 'aliveness' (40-80% success rate). In addition, it iterates the metadata array, the values array, and the keys array, making 3 separate memory accesses. Both of these factors make iteration very slow in comparison.
- `AHashMap` `erase` has to make another lookup, to account for the relocated value. This makes it slower than `OAHashMap`'s erase. 
- `AHashMap`'s `insert` and `lookup` (usually) only needs two cache misses. `OAHashMap` usually needs three cache misses, making it slower for cold access.
- `OAHashMap` needs to relocate values and keys on rehash. `AHashMap` only needs to re-insert metadata.
- `OAHashMap` needs element arrays of full capacity. `AHashMap`'s KeyValue arrays need only be 75% of capacity, saving memory.
- `AHashMap`'s `KeyValue` array may need padding, adding up to 7 bytes per element (`byte` key and `double` value). In this rare case, `AHashMap` will use more memory than `OAHashMap` (but less than 10% added memory). 

Most of the difference between the two decisions are in favour of `AHashMap`. Most notably, access and iteration are favourable for `AHashMap`. This makes it the better choice for most situations.

## Benchmarks

I tested insertion, updating, erasing and access on hot hash maps (cold maps are discussed above, but not tested).
I found that `AHashMap` is favourable to `OAHashMap` in nearly all situations, often more than 5x.

<details>
<summary>Test code</summary>

```c++
for (int size : { 1, 2, 8, 64, 1024, 4096, 20000}) {
		{
			size_t time_ns = 0;
			size_t time_ns1 = 0;
			for (int run = 0; run < 20000000 / size; run++) {
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				AHashMap<Variant, Variant, VariantHasher, VariantComparator> dictionary;
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					dictionary.insert(idx, idx);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					dictionary.insert(idx, idx);
				}
				auto t2 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();

				time_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();
				time_ns1 += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t2 - t1).count();
			}

			std::cout << "insert:" << size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";

			std::cout << "insert existing:" << size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_ns1 / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_ns = 0;
			for (int run = 0; run < 20000000 / size; run++) {
				AHashMap<Variant, Variant, VariantHasher, VariantComparator> dictionary;
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					dictionary.insert(idx, idx);
				}
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					dictionary.erase(idx);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();
			}

			std::cout << "erase:" << size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
		{
			size_t time_ns = 0;
			for (int run = 0; run < 20000000 / size; run++) {
				AHashMap<Variant, Variant, VariantHasher, VariantComparator> dictionary;
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					dictionary.insert(idx, idx);
				}
				size_t total = 0;
				auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				for (int idx = 0; idx < size; idx ++) {
					// Test
					total += dictionary.has(idx);
				}
				auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
				time_ns += std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();

				// Prevent compiling this out.
				volatile_size_t = total;
			}

			std::cout << "get:" << size << std::endl;
			std::cout << time_ns / 1000 / 1000 << "ms\n";
		}
	}
```
</details>

I tested 3 key-value pairs, across a few different sizes:

- **<int16_t, int16_t>** (claimed by @Nazarwadim to favour `OAHashMap`, but [without tests](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/92554#issuecomment-22073810040))
- **<int64_t, int64_t>**
- **<Variant, Variant>**

And there's the raw results, for anyone interested:
- [AHashMap.Variant.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533750/AHashMap.Variant.txt)
- [OAHashMap.int16_t.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533751/OAHashMap.int16_t.txt)
- [OAHashMap.int64_t.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533752/OAHashMap.int64_t.txt)
- [OAHashMap.Variant.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533753/OAHashMap.Variant.txt)
- [AHashMap.int16_t.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533748/AHashMap.int16_t.txt)
- [AHashMap.int64_t.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20533749/AHashMap.int64_t.txt)

We're approaching quite a few numbers now, so I made them into graphs of size vs time spent (lower is better):

![get](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8d5265ef-4b92-42dd-b97d-7bb5c16250ad)

![insert](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7492e535-06ff-4635-a548-e2b1015e6922)

![update](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f3101814-3a06-4372-b87e-740b58c6b88c)

![erase](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0181708c-e92f-46d5-b12d-7d72d426c0ba)

I think it's clear that `AHashMap` should be a net improvement in nearly all situations, without much risk of regression.
